Hi Nan,

Happy vacation!

Here’s my understanding, and we can discuss any details when you are back.

The SOM forcing file should (if I remember) have the mixed layer depth, the “q-flux” (ocean heat transport), mixed-layer temperature and ice fraction. 

I think the best way to do the CAPT runs would be to provide each forecast with a SOM forcing file that has appropriate:
SST or mixed-layer temperature; could be from observation or from obs-forced g-case for initialization time
ice cover; could be from observations or from obs-forced g-case for initialization time
mixed layer depth from the g-case ; preferably for initialization time, but could be climatology
q-flux based on g-case climatology

The entire SOM is just one equation, really (code in cime/src/components/data_comps_mct/docn/src/docn_comp_mod.F90). The SOM temperature is updated:

T(t) = T(t-1) + (SW + LW + SH + LH + MELT - QFLUX - Li*(SNOW+RUNOFF)) * (dt/(cp*rho*h))

Starting with the correct temperature, T(0), and mixed layer depth, h, are important to start the slab with the right heat capacity. The QFLUX matters, but should be a secondary term for our purposes because it should be relatively small and change slowly (so the climatology is fine for that term). 

